-- =====================================================================
-- RRNetBanking — Slice 6 (Profile, credentials, dashboard summary)
--
-- Two things: the self-service profile pages the legacy app had
-- (name.jsp, mobile.jsp, pass.jsp), and the SMS seam its prose promised
-- but never built — "The changed username and password can be send
-- through the sms."
--
-- Plus an OQL view entity for the dashboard, which is where the
-- interesting constraint turned up. See the note on AccountSummary.
-- =====================================================================

/**
 * Per-account dashboard summary: balance, branch, how much activity and
 * when it last happened, in one row per account.
 *
 * WHY THIS CARRIES CustomerLogin AND WHY THAT MATTERS
 *
 * Mendix view entities CAN have associations — you select the associated
 * object's `.ID` and the column becomes an association — and with an
 * association to Banking.Customer this view could be constrained the way
 * every other entity in this app is:
 *
 *   GRANT ... (READ *) WHERE '[Banking.AccountSummary_Customer = ''[%CurrentUser%]'']'
 *
 * MDL cannot express that. Declaring `AccountSummary_Customer:
 * Banking.Customer` parses, but mxcli writes it as an *enumeration*
 * attribute and the build fails with CE1613 "The selected enumeration
 * 'Banking.Customer' no longer exists" — while simultaneously reporting
 * that the entity and the OQL query reference an association that does
 * not exist, which is Mendix telling us the concept is real. Every other
 * spelling (`Reference(...)`, `Association(...)`, a trailing ASSOCIATION
 * keyword) is a parse error, and a separate CREATE ASSOCIATION is
 * rejected outright with CE6771 "It is not possible to create
 * associations to/from View Entities."
 *
 * It turned out to be worse than that. mxcli cannot reference this view
 * ANYWHERE once it has been created:
 *
 *   GRANT ... ON Banking.AccountSummary   -> entity not found
 *   RETURNS LIST OF Banking.AccountSummary -> entity not found for return type
 *   SHOW ENTITIES IN Banking               -> does not list it
 *
 * A view entity is effectively write-only in MDL. It builds clean, and
 * Mendix is perfectly happy with it — but nothing in MDL can grant access
 * to it or read from it, so no page and no microflow this tool writes can
 * use it.
 *
 * This definition is therefore STAGED WORK, not dead weight: it is valid,
 * it builds, and finishing it in Studio Pro is two steps — add the
 * association by selecting c.ID, and add the access rule. Until then the
 * dashboard is fed by DS_MyDashboard, which aggregates over the
 * already-secured Account and Transaction entities.
 *
 * There is deliberately no customer column on it. The obvious way to
 * carry one — `cast(c.Name as string(200)) as CustomerLogin` over a join
 * to Banking.Customer — does not work either: `cast()` is not in the MDL
 * grammar, and selecting the inherited `c.Name` fails CE6770 whatever
 * length is declared (String(200) and String(unlimited) both). Since the
 * view cannot be granted or read from MDL regardless, chasing that was
 * not worth it — the aggregate columns below are the part worth keeping.
 *
 * Two more traps found while getting this far, both quiet:
 *   * `--` comments are not accepted inside the OQL body. The parse fails,
 *     `exec` then does not run, and if you are grepping the output for a
 *     success line you see nothing and can easily read it as success. It
 *     is how an earlier attempt "passed" while the view had actually been
 *     dropped and never recreated.
 *   * `CREATE OR MODIFY` on a view does not prune members the OQL no
 *     longer produces. After a bad edit it has to be DROPped and recreated
 *     or CE6770 persists.
 *
 * Note the join to Branch is INNER and to Transaction is LEFT: a new
 * account with no ledger must still appear, with a count of 0.
 *
 * CustomerLogin is CAST rather than passed through. System.User.Name is
 * String(unlimited), and declaring the column String(200) against a
 * pass-through fails the build with CE6770 "View Entity is out of sync
 * with the OQL Query".
 *
 * Do not put `--` comments inside the OQL body: the MDL parser does not
 * accept them there, and the resulting parse failure is easy to miss
 * because `exec` then simply does not run.
 */
CREATE OR MODIFY VIEW ENTITY Banking.AccountSummary (
  AccountNumber: Long,
  AccountLabel: String(60),
  Balance: Decimal,
  BranchName: String(100),
  TransactionCount: Integer,
  LastActivityOn: DateTime
) AS (
  select
    a.AccountNumber as AccountNumber,
    a.AccountLabel as AccountLabel,
    a.Balance as Balance,
    b.BranchName as BranchName,
    count(t.Reference) as TransactionCount,
    max(t.BookedOn) as LastActivityOn
  from Banking.Account as a
  inner join Banking.Account_Branch/Banking.Branch as b
  left join Banking.Transaction_Account/Banking.Transaction as t
  group by a.AccountNumber, a.AccountLabel, a.Balance, b.BranchName
);

/**
 * What the dashboard shows per account.
 *
 * Non-persistent, filled by DS_MyDashboard. This exists because the view
 * entity above cannot be read or secured from MDL — see its note. The
 * shape deliberately matches the view's columns, so switching the
 * datasource over once the view is finished in Studio Pro is a change to
 * one microflow and nothing else.
 */
CREATE OR MODIFY NON-PERSISTENT ENTITY Banking.DashboardCard (
  AccountNumber: Long,
  AccountLabel: String(60),
  Balance: Decimal,
  BranchName: String(100),
  TransactionCount: Integer,
  LastActivityOn: DateTime
)
COMMENT 'One dashboard row per account. Mirrors the AccountSummary view.';
/

/**
 * How a notification would be delivered.
 */
CREATE OR MODIFY ENUMERATION Banking.ENUM_NotificationChannel (
  SMS 'SMS',
  Email 'Email'
);
/

/**
 * A notification the bank owes the customer.
 *
 * THIS IS A SEAM, NOT A GATEWAY. Nothing here sends anything: rows are
 * queued with IsSent = false and stay that way. The legacy system
 * promised SMS on credential changes and shipped no SMS code at all, so
 * this makes the promise explicit and inspectable instead of silently
 * absent. Wiring a real provider means one scheduled microflow that picks
 * up unsent rows and calls it — the model does not change.
 */
CREATE OR MODIFY PERSISTENT ENTITY Banking.Notification (
  Reference: AutoNumber DEFAULT 600000001,
  Channel: Enumeration(Banking.ENUM_NotificationChannel),
  Recipient: String(100),
  Message: String(400),
  QueuedOn: DateTime NOT NULL,
  IsSent: Boolean,
  SentOn: DateTime
)
INDEX (QueuedOn)
COMMENT 'A queued customer notification. Never actually sent — see the entity documentation.';
/

CREATE OR MODIFY ASSOCIATION Banking.Notification_Customer
  FROM Banking.Notification TO Banking.Customer
  TYPE Reference
  DELETE_BEHAVIOR CASCADE
  COMMENT 'Customer the notification is for.';
/

/**
 * Form state for the profile page. Legacy: name.jsp and mobile.jsp,
 * which posted a single field each to the Controller.
 */
CREATE OR MODIFY NON-PERSISTENT ENTITY Banking.ProfileForm (
  FullName: String(200),
  Address: String(200),
  MobileNumber: String(15)
)
COMMENT 'Form state for the profile page. Not stored.';
/

/**
 * Form state for changing the password. Legacy: pass.jsp, whose servlet
 * handler was called emailu() — a copy-paste artefact.
 *
 * Plain String, not HashedString: this mirrors
 * Administration.AccountPasswordData, which is how the platform's own
 * change-password page carries a password before commit. Mendix hashes
 * it when it is assigned to the user entity's Password member.
 */
CREATE OR MODIFY NON-PERSISTENT ENTITY Banking.PasswordForm (
  NewPassword: String(200),
  ConfirmPassword: String(200)
)
COMMENT 'Form state for changing the password. Not stored.';
/
